00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.430
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.040
Yeah.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.630
This is something a little
bit near and dear to my heart.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.630
I quite like it.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:57.367
We want to talk about-- how
do we want to set the stage?

00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:58.950
Generally, this sort
of stuff comes up

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.050
when you want to do either
asynchronous processing,

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.990
or maybe you have
disparate systems

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.115
that want to be able to
communicate with each other

00:05:06.115 --> 00:05:08.070
through some sort
of messaging system.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.980
What options are available
on Google Cloud Platform

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.590
to let you do that?

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.141
And why would you
choose one over another?

00:05:18.141 --> 00:05:21.050
And I think-- to set the stage--
I think, really, we're looking

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.090
at two particular pieces.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.000
One of which is Task queues,
which have been around

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:26.700
for a long time.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.824
They've been in App Engine.

00:05:27.824 --> 00:05:30.510
And something that's relatively
newer-ish, or at least

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.810
relatively newer
released publicly,

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:34.970
is Cloud Pub/Sub, which
is pretty exciting.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:37.308
So I think we'll end up
talking about both of those.

00:05:37.308 --> 00:05:38.433
Have you worked with those?

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I've definitely
used Task queues before.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:46.180
Task Queues have been
around for a very long time.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.580
So I've used it for many
demos that I've done.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:49.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And it's
really simple to use.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.631
It's beautiful.

00:05:51.631 --> 00:05:53.380
It's very well integrated
with App Engine.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:54.880
So if you're doing
App Engine, it's

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.310
probably the way to go with it.

00:05:57.310 --> 00:05:59.980
And then I also used
Pub/Sub up in my last demo.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.790
The Google Cloud
Spin uses Pub/Sub

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.780
to communicate to send tasks
across all the workers.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:07.607
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:06:07.607 --> 00:06:09.190
Why don't we explain
what both of them

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:11.845
are and we can
continue on from there?

00:06:11.845 --> 00:06:13.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.352
Since I've used
Task queues more--

00:06:16.352 --> 00:06:17.310
MARK MANDEL: Go for it.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --I will
explain what Task queues is.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.500
Basically, Task queues are just
a very basic implementation.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.200
And by basic, I mean very simple
to use and very down to earth.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:29.930
It's nothing really fancy.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.840
Basically, what you can
do is you have queues.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.260
And these queues-- you
can think about them

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.380
like pipes-- like Linux pipes.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:44.750
Or just a first in, first
out thing on the Cloud,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.860
where you can send messages
on one side and then,

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.180
on the other side,
you can receive them.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:48.890
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you
have two ways of doing that.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.010
Sending those messages can
be done through a REST API

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:57.220
or directly from App
Engine, you have some--

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.720
MARK MANDEL: You
have a nice client.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:06:58.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.532
You have a very nice client
that you can use very easily.

00:07:01.532 --> 00:07:03.990
And then, on the other side,
the way you receive them-- you

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.290
have two choices.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.560
One is what we call a pool task.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.840
And a pool task is basically
when you are asking-- OK, I'm

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.140
ready to receive a task.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:14.160
Give me one task.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.120
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And once
you're done traiting that task,

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.730
you send out a message, which
is an acknowledgement, saying,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.010
hey, I'm done with that task.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:24.030
You can delete it.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:24.739
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:07:24.739 --> 00:07:26.779
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
actually really powerful.

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:28.860
Because that way,
if for some reason--

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:32.760
you start traiting that
task-- and for some reason,

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.627
the machine just crashes,
you don't lose that task.

00:07:35.627 --> 00:07:36.960
MARK MANDEL: It can still retry.

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:37.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.719
It will be retried later
on, after some time out time

00:07:39.719 --> 00:07:40.260
that we have.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:44.500
So that's very simple and
it's very often what you want.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:46.820
You can also have something
which is the opposite way.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:50.560
Rather than the workers
asking for a new task,

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:56.180
is actually the task being
sent directly to the workers.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:56.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.991
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
that works really well

00:07:58.991 --> 00:08:00.490
if you have, for
instance, something

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:04.244
like some kind of load balancer
and a bunch of machines behind.

00:08:04.244 --> 00:08:05.910
MARK MANDEL: Especially
like App Engine.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.260
If you're using App Engine,
that works perfectly.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:08.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: On App
Engine, it works really well.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.690
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
But then, you still

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.310
have the fact that if you
tried to send that task

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.900
and you respond to that
task with something else,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.520
like 200 or 202--
basically, an OK

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.641
HTTP code-- then that task
will be retried later on.

00:08:23.641 --> 00:08:24.390
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
when we retry things,

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.026
there's always this
exponential back up-- back out.

00:08:30.026 --> 00:08:31.650
MARK MANDEL: One of
the things I really

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.183
like about Task Queues is
it's a configuration file.

00:08:34.183 --> 00:08:36.434
It's a YAML file-- much like
everything we always do--

00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:37.891
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, that's true.

00:08:37.891 --> 00:08:39.970
MARK MANDEL: --that allows
you to say how quickly

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.512
and how often you want
things to get processed.

00:08:43.512 --> 00:08:44.970
And also, you have
a lot of control

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.310
over how you want to retry--
how many times, what sort of

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.480
back off, all that stuff.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.842
So you have a lot of
control over all the Task

00:08:52.842 --> 00:08:53.800
Queues in every system.

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:55.380
And you can have more than one.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.340
It's quite powerful.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.256
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:08:57.256 --> 00:08:58.882
You can have as many
tasks as you want.

00:08:58.882 --> 00:09:00.589
MARK MANDEL: And the
other thing I really

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:02.320
like about task queues--
with push tasks,

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.284
specifically-- you can
defer them for later.

00:09:04.284 --> 00:09:05.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:06.310
MARK MANDEL: Which
is really nice, too.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:07.960
You want to run this
in half an hour,

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:10.840
or you want to run this the next
day, or something like that,

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.145
but do it's as a one-off thing,
that's also quite powerful.

00:09:14.145 --> 00:09:15.820
It's pretty nice.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:16.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:21.060
I've seen it used very
often for two different use

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.980
cases that are very different,
but work very well with task

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:25.480
queues.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:26.200
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.741
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The first one being,

00:09:27.741 --> 00:09:31.290
you have some list of
things that you have to do.

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.770
Imagine, for instance,
you have some forum

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.264
and you have to
moderate the new posts.

00:09:37.264 --> 00:09:38.180
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:39.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Those posts--
you can put them inside

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:40.740
of a task queue.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.660
And then the web page
itself-- maybe even

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:46.260
JavaScript-- you can access.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.420
Give me the next task.

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.570
You present it and then you
have everything done there.

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:54.820
So it's very simple to
do and it works very well

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.520
with a pool mode.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.370
And the other one
is when you want

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.290
to do something
at it a bit later

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.800
because that way, your
request will finish faster.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:04.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:05.840
So like a performance increase.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:06.370
That makes a lot of sense.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:07.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:08.708
For instance, if
you have something

00:10:08.708 --> 00:10:13.040
where you receive a request,
and as a result of that request,

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:14.800
you have to send an email.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.110
Sending an email might take
some time, so you don't--

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.370
MARK MANDEL: Maybe you have
some attachments or something.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:19.286
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:19.286 --> 00:10:22.510
Maybe you need to go find some
images or something like that.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.650
So very often,
it's better-- what

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.310
you want to do is just
to create that task

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.010
and say that will be done as
soon as possible, but not right

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:30.510
now.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.000
And then just return
a 200 to the customer.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:33.750
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.160
That makes a lot of sense.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:35.740
That's a lot of sense.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:36.240
All right.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:36.750
Cool.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.100
So Task Queues sound
pretty powerful.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.811
They sound like they've got a
lot of really great features.

00:10:41.811 --> 00:10:43.810
So I guess it falls on
me to talk about Pub/Sub.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:44.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I think so, yeah.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:45.781
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, great.

00:10:45.781 --> 00:10:47.810
I've worked with Pub/Sub before.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:49.760
Pub/Sub is pretty
much, actually,

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.184
exactly as he explained.

00:10:52.184 --> 00:10:54.350
If anyone's looked at the
software patent of Publish

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:58.570
Subscribe, it is exactly that.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:02.445
You have publishers which
can send out messages.

00:11:02.445 --> 00:11:04.070
And they send those
messages to topics.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.360
And this is exactly what
is called inside Pub/Sub.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.810
Those topics, essentially--
everything about Pub/Sub

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:11.566
is HTTP, which I think is great.

00:11:11.566 --> 00:11:12.690
Makes things really simple.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.140
It's actually specifically
over HTTPS, so it's secure.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:18.670
Publishers can push messages
to JSON packets straight

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:21.270
to topics that can be created.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:22.710
Any number of
publishers, that is.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.550
And then, any number
of subscribers

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:27.490
can subscribe to those topics
and get those messages.

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:31.560
So you can do fan in, fan out,
so many to one, one to many--

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.290
all that good stuff.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.207
So you can build some really
nice, decoupled architecture.

00:11:36.207 --> 00:11:38.540
Pretty much like what you can
do with which Task Queues,

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:41.010
but you have this
opportunity here where

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.469
you can have many people
listening, or many subscribers

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:45.010
listening, to a
single message, which

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:46.050
you can't do with Task Queues.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:47.466
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's very nice.

00:11:47.466 --> 00:11:49.962
Basically, with Pub/Sub,
you're able to do broadcast.

00:11:49.962 --> 00:11:50.670
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.175
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you
cannot really do that with Task

00:11:53.175 --> 00:11:53.370
Queues.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:53.760
That's true.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:54.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.820
It gives you a little bit
more flexibility that way.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.670
The really cool
thing about this--

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:01.930
while it is relatively new,
as well, it's pretty battle

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:03.071
tested.

00:12:03.071 --> 00:12:04.570
I actually started
using it way back

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:07.110
when, when it first came
out and it was really new,

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.479
but it was rock solid.

00:12:08.479 --> 00:12:10.270
Basically, it's because
it's the same thing

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.810
that powers things like ads
and Hangouts within the site

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:13.440
Google.

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:15.273
And you know anything
that powers ads inside

00:12:15.273 --> 00:12:16.485
Google-- that has to work.

00:12:16.485 --> 00:12:18.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That has
the work of all the time.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:18.740
MARK MANDEL: All the time.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.031
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And very fast.

00:12:20.031 --> 00:12:21.580
MARK MANDEL: All the time.

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:25.780
Much like Task Queues,
it's guaranteed

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.040
to be at least once to show up.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:29.430
So it's persistent storage.

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
actually very important.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.390
It will not happen
very often that you

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.030
receive the same task twice.

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:36.130
MARK MANDEL: I don't
think I've ever seen it,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:36.920
but it could happen.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:37.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
have never seen it,

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:40.220
but you should be ready
for that happening.

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:45.670
So it's important to be able to
treat a second request as just

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.000
not important.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.040
You can either ignore it or
you do the treatment twice.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.980
But that doesn't take
you into a wrong state.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:53.730
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:54.780
Make sure it's [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:55.080
Make sure it--

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:55.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:56.910
That's the word-- [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:02.330
MARK MANDEL: That's
super important.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:04.377
It's also worth noting,
as well, Pub/Sub-- also,

00:13:04.377 --> 00:13:05.960
again, like you can
with Task Queues--

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.750
there's a lot of
similarities here.

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:11.070
You can do both push and pull,
very much like Task Queues.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:12.630
So you might use
push when you want

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:15.740
to send it directly to somebody
who needs to receive it.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:19.730
It fires an HTTP request
with the data straight in it.

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.300
But then, also, you can
do pull requests, as well.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.760
So you can say, I want to
grab the last 10 or 15.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:25.970
What's the max number?

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:27.049
And it sends it down.

00:13:27.049 --> 00:13:28.590
That can be quite
useful if you don't

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.490
want to be order
scaling all the time.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:32.340
You want to say, I've
got three machines

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.420
and I just want
to keep it going.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.650
But the nice thing is
that it has integration

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:37.735
with cloud monitoring.

00:13:37.735 --> 00:13:38.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:41.440
MARK MANDEL: So if you want
to do some auto scaling based

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:44.290
on how much stuff is
in your Pub/Sub queue--

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:46.940
or in your Pub/Sub topic, I
should say-- you can do that.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.399
And that integration
is quite nice.

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:49.314
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:49.314 --> 00:13:52.000
And that's something that you
cannot do out of the box with

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:52.660
Task Queues.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.660
It's something that,
maybe, you could implement,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:58.030
but it's definitely not as
simple to do as with Pub/Sub.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:58.780
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:13:59.280
Yeah.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:13:59.960
Yeah.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:02.300
But the other flipside
is Task Queues

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.380
give you all the
knobs to play with.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.560
To say, I want to go faster
here or back off more there.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:10.170
Whereas Pub/Sub is
more rudimentary.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:12.340
It has that retry mechanisms.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:13.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:16.360
MARK MANDEL: But it's just
like, this is the way it works.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:18.110
It doesn't-- you
don't have like, oh,

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:20.840
I want it to back off once
and then go back 10 seconds,

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:22.400
then 15, then 20.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:25.140
You It's like you're on
Pub/Sub's dime on how

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:25.730
that works.

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:29.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's where
the task queues give you

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.510
more fine grained tuning.

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:30.800
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:31.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
there's things that

00:14:31.690 --> 00:14:32.981
are actually quite interesting.

00:14:32.981 --> 00:14:35.650
For instance, you can
actually have throttling.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:39.170
You could say, oh, in this
task-- only for push tasks,

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:39.670
though.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:39.960
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:40.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Because if you do pull--

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.040
MARK MANDEL: Pull,
it doesn't matter.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.373
FRANCESC CAMPOY: As soon as
you pull, you got new tasks.

00:14:44.373 --> 00:14:46.450
But when you push tasks,
you could say, oh,

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:49.500
yeah I don't want to send more
than 10 messages per second.

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:52.980
And this can be really powerful,
because if you have a traffic

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.750
peak, your task queue will
just moderate that traffic peak

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:58.990
and just keep the tasks
for a little bit later.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:14:59.740
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:04.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So your users
will experience some lag,

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.220
but your system will continue
working with that [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:07.720
glitch.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:07.980
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.045
Whereas with Pub/Sub, if
you wanted to do that,

00:15:10.045 --> 00:15:11.545
you'd have to do
it as a pull queue.

00:15:11.545 --> 00:15:12.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:13.988
MARK MANDEL: You'd have
to regulate it yourself

00:15:13.988 --> 00:15:18.180
and say this is how often I want
to be processing each message.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.220
The other thing with
Pub/Sub-- regardless

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.240
of whether you do
push or pull, you

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:23.810
have to send back
an ACK message.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:27.370
You have to acknowledge
that you're done.

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:28.370
That's really important.

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:30.150
And it's a bit simpler that way.

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.227
I don't know-- maybe in my mind.

00:15:32.227 --> 00:15:34.560
Regardless of whether you
push or pull, as long as you--

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.000
once you're done, you have to
say, hey, I'm done with it.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:38.360
You specify deadline with it.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:39.776
So you're like,
this is how long I

00:15:39.776 --> 00:15:42.240
think it's going to take
when you set up everything.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:43.850
But then, once you say
ACK, it's like, oh, cool.

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:44.480
I'll delete it now.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.000
I'm good to go.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.291
Otherwise, yeah, then you
get into that retry mechanism

00:15:47.291 --> 00:15:50.510
and everything works from there.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:51.010
But yeah.

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:54.040
I mean, one thing I
really love about Pub/Sub

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:57.650
is that it doesn't require
any special type of transport

00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:58.260
layer.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:00.427
You don't need any
like custom clients,

00:16:00.427 --> 00:16:02.510
you don't need to be talking
over special sockets,

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:03.550
or anything like that.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:04.790
It's all HTTP.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:05.794
It's all JSON packets.

00:16:05.794 --> 00:16:06.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:08.120
MARK MANDEL: It's all,
pretty much, plain text

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:09.200
going over the wire.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.449
So anything that can talk that,
which is pretty much anything,

00:16:13.449 --> 00:16:13.990
that's great.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:18.640
And so then you can
do inner communication

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.060
between whatever you like.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:20.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:21.480
MARK MANDEL: And
it's really flexible

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:23.640
and you never-- if you
want to add something

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.452
extra-- for example-- what's the
great example I'm thinking of?

00:16:27.452 --> 00:16:29.660
You want to do something
Task Queue-ish, for example.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:31.670
You were talking about--
say you were doing-- I want

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:32.900
to process this image later.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.310
You could put that
into a Pub/Sub topic,

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:37.010
much like you could
with task queues.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:38.600
But then, maybe later
on, you're like, oh,

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:40.225
what I also want to
do is I want to log

00:16:40.225 --> 00:16:41.560
every time I do that, as well.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:42.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:43.700
MARK MANDEL: So then
you go, OK, that's fine.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:45.390
I will just add
another subscription

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:46.630
to my Pub/Sub thing.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:47.130
Same thing.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:49.360
Don't have to change
any of my other code.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:50.990
And then I'm like, oh, I've
just got another listener

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.989
to this particular topic
when the image comes in

00:16:52.989 --> 00:16:53.950
and then I'll log it.

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:55.350
And then maybe you're
like, oh, actually,

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.060
I also want to do this
other thing with an image.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.430
And you can continue
on from there.

00:16:58.430 --> 00:17:00.370
And that's a really
powerful medium, I think.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:00.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:01.180
That's really nice.

00:17:01.180 --> 00:17:02.870
The fact that you
can basically have

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:07.540
a much generic architecture,
where you can add

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.532
stuff without
modifying any code.

00:17:09.532 --> 00:17:10.490
That's really powerful.

00:17:10.490 --> 00:17:12.505
MARK MANDEL: That's
super, super powerful.

00:17:12.505 --> 00:17:14.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What you
were saying about-- yeah,

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:17.790
there's no specific transport
that is required or anything.

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:21.163
Just like HTTP, after
HTTPS with JSON.

00:17:21.163 --> 00:17:23.079
That's actually also the
case for Task Queues.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:24.390
MARK MANDEL: That is true.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:25.820
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So yeah.

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:26.849
Don't get too excited.

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:28.560
MARK MANDEL: I feel like we're
having this battle here your

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.560
on Task Queue's side and
I'm on Pub/Sub's side.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.726
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I actually
like Pub/Sub a lot, too.

00:17:32.726 --> 00:17:34.824
But there's also something
really important,

00:17:34.824 --> 00:17:36.240
which we have not
mentioned, which

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:39.610
is there is absolutely
nothing that you

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.502
need to do to get Pub/Sub
or Task Queues working.

00:17:42.502 --> 00:17:43.960
You don't need to
install anything.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:44.720
MARK MANDEL: No, that's true.

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:46.845
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You don't
need to manage anything.

00:17:46.845 --> 00:17:49.370
It's fully managed services
that are a part of Google Cloud.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:50.120
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:52.245
If you need to use them,
you just start using them.

00:17:52.245 --> 00:17:52.810
That's it.

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:52.980
MARK MANDEL: That's it.

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:53.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There's nothing to do.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:54.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.040
That's a really good point.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:59.550
Why don't we go through some
of the feature comparisons

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:03.360
between the two, so people
get a bit of an idea of maybe

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:04.900
which bits they
might want to use

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.149
in their application for what?

00:18:06.149 --> 00:18:09.755
Because there is a
lot of overlap there.

00:18:09.755 --> 00:18:11.880
We're going to put this
table up in the show notes.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:12.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:12.890
MARK MANDEL:
Francesc is nodding.

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:13.640
He agrees with me.

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.430
Good.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:16.600
So yeah.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:18.370
We can-- you can
dig into it further.

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.140
But I think it's
worth going into.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:21.360
Because there is overlap.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:22.818
I can see why people
would be like,

00:18:22.818 --> 00:18:24.884
oh, I'm going to go
this way or go that way.

00:18:24.884 --> 00:18:26.800
I can think of what my
rule of thumb might be.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:29.077
Maybe it's different
from your rule of thumb.

00:18:29.077 --> 00:18:30.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You'll have to say it

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:31.960
so I can agree or disagree.

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:34.540
MARK MANDEL: I probably lean
more towards Pub/Sub these days

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:35.352
than task queues.

00:18:35.352 --> 00:18:36.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:38.270
MARK MANDEL: Just
because it allows

00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.234
you more flexibility to
add extra things to it.

00:18:41.234 --> 00:18:42.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.733
MARK MANDEL: But I
do put some caveats

00:18:43.733 --> 00:18:47.620
on that, in terms
of if you do need

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:52.243
that fine grained control over
processing speed or retries,

00:18:52.243 --> 00:18:53.740
could go task queues.

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:57.700
You could also just do
pull requests on Pub/Sub.

00:18:57.700 --> 00:18:58.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:00.299
For me, it's a
little bit different.

00:19:00.299 --> 00:19:02.840
For me, it depends
on what I'm doing.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.270
If I'm building
an App Engine app,

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:07.287
task queues is very
well integrated with it.

00:19:07.287 --> 00:19:08.370
MARK MANDEL: That is true.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I will
probably go with Task Queues.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:13.820
If I'm not using Task-- if I'm
not using App Engine-- if I'm

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.860
doing something on Compute
Engine, which is my own code,

00:19:17.860 --> 00:19:20.260
especially if it's involving
something which is not

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:22.960
a part of the cloud--
like internet of things--

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:26.010
and you have many devices
that are around, communication

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:29.610
with those over
Pub/Sub, that's going

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:32.820
to be much, much better to do.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:35.440
Except for one
little thing, which

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.420
is if you need
something-- there's

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:40.880
something that Pub/Sub doesn't
offer that Task Queue does,

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:43.920
which is do this
in half an hour.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:45.050
MARK MANDEL: That is true.

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:45.670
That is true.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The fact
that-- and that can be quite--

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:49.290
MARK MANDEL: That's
quite powerful.

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:50.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Quite powerful.

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:51.970
You can do something
like, oh, yeah,

00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:53.110
I'm receiving this request.

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:53.860
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:55.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Check in
half an hour if this happened.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.860
If it didn't happen,
do this other thing.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:58.580
MARK MANDEL: I've worked it out.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:00.950
You create a Task Queue that
then creates a Pub/Sub message.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:01.449
Done.

00:20:01.449 --> 00:20:02.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Aw.

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:03.170
Probably.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:03.520
OK.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:04.000
MARK MANDEL: Maybe.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:04.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:04.840
You could do that, too.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:05.690
MARK MANDEL: It really depends.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:07.720
Again, you probably need
simplicity, as well.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.534
But that's fair enough.

00:20:10.534 --> 00:20:11.200
No, that's cool.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.241
And there's one other cute
trick that Task Queues

00:20:13.241 --> 00:20:14.800
do that we actually--
I'll be devil's

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:16.740
advocate against myself.

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:20.080
If you're on App Engine,
you can do transactions

00:20:20.080 --> 00:20:23.450
within Datastore that wrap
around your task queues.

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:25.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh,
that's very powerful.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:25.920
MARK MANDEL: Which
is quite cool.

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:28.797
So if you're like, I'm going
to put some data in Datastore,

00:20:28.797 --> 00:20:30.630
and then I want to
create a task, basically,

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:33.460
against that data, you can
wrap that up in a transaction

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:35.620
so that they're one in the same.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:38.610
So if one of them-- if it
fails, it all falls down.

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:41.190
You could probably
replicate it using Pub/Sub

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:42.582
and your own [INAUDIBLE] stuff.

00:20:42.582 --> 00:20:44.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That'd
be quite hard, I guess.

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:44.830
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:47.316
You could probably do it in
some ways, like check it.

00:20:47.316 --> 00:20:49.440
But the fact that those
are integrated quite nicely

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:49.930
together--

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:50.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's very nice.

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:51.490
MARK MANDEL: It's pretty cool.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:52.240
It is pretty cool.

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:57.175
We mentioned the feature matrix
before we went off there.

00:20:57.175 --> 00:20:58.008
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:20:58.008 --> 00:20:58.980
Yeah.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.320
Cool.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:03.760
The first feature is
you can pull and push

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:05.810
via-- no, pull via API.

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:06.650
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:07.180
That works.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:08.721
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Which is a rest API.

00:21:08.721 --> 00:21:10.420
We also have clients,
but those are--

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:11.780
MARK MANDEL: They both work.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:12.696
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:12.696 --> 00:21:14.040
Both Task Queues and Pub/Sub.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.790
What about pushing
via web hooks?

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:18.912
Actually, pushing to
web hooks, basically?

00:21:18.912 --> 00:21:19.620
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:20.860
They both have that, obviously.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:23.484
FRANCESC CAMPOY: They both have
that under one condition, which

00:21:23.484 --> 00:21:24.110
is HTTPS.

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:25.040
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:26.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which is
always-- you should always--

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:28.970
MARK MANDEL: You should do
that anyway, so that's fine.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:30.053
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But yeah.

00:21:30.053 --> 00:21:32.610
What about the size of every
single message that gets sent?

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.234
MARK MANDEL: That's
an interesting one.

00:21:34.234 --> 00:21:37.010
Task queues have a
limited 100K per message.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:37.922
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:21:37.922 --> 00:21:40.130
MARK MANDEL: Pub/Sub's a
little bit more interesting.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:45.030
It's actually 10 megabytes
per published request.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:50.070
But you can send up to 1,000
messages within that request.

00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:52.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you
could send 1,000 messages

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:56.537
and every single message
could have 1,000 bytes.

00:21:56.537 --> 00:21:57.870
MARK MANDEL: Get the math right.

00:21:57.870 --> 00:21:58.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:21:59.240
OK.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.915
So you could send 1K
messages of 1K size.

00:22:01.915 --> 00:22:03.290
MARK MANDEL:
Something like that.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:03.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which is 1 Meg.

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:04.300
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:22:04.300 --> 00:22:05.190
I think that math sounds right.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.314
Yeah, so you could do that.

00:22:06.314 --> 00:22:09.000
Or you could send one message
that is up to 10 megabytes.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:09.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:11.490
MARK MANDEL: So you have
some flexibility there,

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:12.580
which is pretty cool.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:14.205
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
important to tell

00:22:14.205 --> 00:22:18.230
that-- to say that even though
you can send 10 Megs, Pub/Sub

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:21.370
and Task Queues-- in general,
messaging systems are not

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.580
really built to handle
really big size of files.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:24.330
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.470
You probably should
store big data

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:28.945
somewhere persistent, like
storage or Datastore or Cloud

00:22:28.945 --> 00:22:29.445
SQL.

00:22:29.445 --> 00:22:29.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:31.350
And [INAUDIBLE] is
the path to that.

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:32.308
Or something like that.

00:22:32.308 --> 00:22:34.520
It's much better than just
sending big files inside.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:35.870
That's not a very good idea.

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:36.620
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:37.890
I agree 100%.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.420
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What about
the delivery guarantee?

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.662
Am I sure that I will
receive those messages?

00:22:42.662 --> 00:22:43.870
MARK MANDEL: I would hope so.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:45.225
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:45.225 --> 00:22:48.150
And again, that's the
little difference.

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:52.070
You will receive that message
and probably only once.

00:22:52.070 --> 00:22:55.340
But, maybe, you might
receive it twice.

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:59.420
So your actions should be
[INAUDIBLE] just in case

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:00.330
that arrives.

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:02.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
have batch inserts--

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:09.110
being able to insert more than
one task or message at a time.

00:23:09.110 --> 00:23:09.840
Can you do that?

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.089
MARK MANDEL: You can only
do that in App Engine, which

00:23:12.089 --> 00:23:12.780
is interesting.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.886
The REST API-- if you're
outside App Engine

00:23:14.886 --> 00:23:16.260
and you want to
use the REST API,

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:17.860
you can only send
one task at a time.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
being for Task Queues.

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:19.110
OK.

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:19.900
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
for Task Queues.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:21.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.260
But on Pub/Sub, you can
do whatever you want.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:23.570
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:25.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So there's
no limitation there.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:26.670
MARK MANDEL: Well,
you have to send it

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:28.770
under 10 megabytes and
up to 1,000 messages.

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:29.686
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:23:29.686 --> 00:23:31.240
But you can send
as many messages

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:35.440
as you want, regardless of
where you're sending them from.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:37.600
Can you have multiple receivers?

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.960
In a Task Queue, if you
create only one task queue,

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.690
and I send one
message, how many times

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.160
will that message be received?

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:45.910
MARK MANDEL: Once.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:46.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, OK.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:47.630
MARK MANDEL: Just once.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:48.150
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just once.

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:48.300
That's it.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:49.020
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:50.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But on
Pub/Sub, there's actually

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:52.156
not that limitation either.

00:23:52.156 --> 00:23:55.880
Because it will be received
once per subscription.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:56.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.750
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And you can
add more subscription per topic.

00:23:58.750 --> 00:23:59.460
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:00.918
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Basically, you can

00:24:00.918 --> 00:24:04.120
have-- you have a little bit of
best of both worlds, where you

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:07.120
can have per subscription,
it will be received

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:08.380
by only one of the receivers.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:09.130
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:11.450
Topics can have up to
10,000 subscriptions.

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:12.950
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Which is quite big.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:14.000
MARK MANDEL: It seems
reasonable amount.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:14.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's a-- yeah.

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:17.058
10,000-- yeah, I don't think
I've ever done something

00:24:17.058 --> 00:24:18.120
with that many machines.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.120
Well, other than when
I was working for ads.

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:21.994
MARK MANDEL: There you go.

00:24:21.994 --> 00:24:23.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
about the maximum number

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:25.420
of messages per second?

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:27.670
MARK MANDEL: You were looking
at this for Task Queues.

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:28.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:29.740
Do you want to try
and explain that?

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:30.573
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:24:30.573 --> 00:24:33.300
So I'm going to go
first with Pub/Sub.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:34.090
There's no limit.

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:34.800
MARK MANDEL: There's no limit.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:35.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just go for it.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:36.570
There's no limit on that.

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:37.320
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:40.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just
go crazy with that.

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:43.312
With Task Queues, there's a
limitation, which is first,

00:24:43.312 --> 00:24:44.520
you have to activate billing.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:46.880
If you have the billing
activated, that won't work.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:50.720
But then you can go up to
1 billion messages per day,

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:52.120
which is quite big.

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:52.700
MARK MANDEL: Seems reasonable.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:53.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:55.050
And the recommendation
is you should not

00:24:55.050 --> 00:25:00.079
go over 250 messages
per second per queue.

00:25:00.079 --> 00:25:02.370
So you have multiple queues,
that doesn't really count.

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:03.710
MARK MANDEL: So you can
shard it if you need to.

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:04.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, you can
shard over multiple queues.

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:05.911
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:25:05.911 --> 00:25:07.440
OK.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:10.244
This seems to handle, I think,
probably most people's load.

00:25:10.244 --> 00:25:11.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.990
If you have like one
billion messages per day.

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:17.220
I have not made the maths
to know how many messages

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:18.510
per second, but that's a lot.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:18.600
MARK MANDEL: A lot.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:19.110
I'd go with a lot.

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:19.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Let's go with a lot.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:21.980
MARK MANDEL: Let's
go with a lot.

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:24.070
We were talking about
throttling before.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:25.022
Yeah.

00:25:25.022 --> 00:25:27.620
Task Queues has built in
throttling so you have control

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:28.625
within that YAML file.

00:25:28.625 --> 00:25:30.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
For push queues.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:30.720
MARK MANDEL: For push queues.

00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:31.250
Yeah.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:33.337
But you always do pull
and control it yourself.

00:25:33.337 --> 00:25:34.212
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:25:34.212 --> 00:25:36.190
MARK MANDEL: Though, with
Pub/Sub, if you're going to do

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:37.864
throttling, you need
to do it yourself.

00:25:37.864 --> 00:25:38.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:40.417
Which is actually
not that complicated.

00:25:40.417 --> 00:25:41.250
I've done it before.

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:44.340
Basically, what you do is
every single receiver--

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:47.590
every single worker-- it will
just sleep for two seconds

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:49.050
before going for the next task.

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:49.550
That's it.

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:50.050
MARK MANDEL: That's it.

00:25:50.050 --> 00:25:50.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
that simple, really.

00:25:50.860 --> 00:25:52.390
MARK MANDEL: Or you
could just keep it going.

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:54.230
If you're just happy-- I mean,
you're paying for the machine

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:54.730
anyway.

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:55.520
You've got an OVM.

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.062
You might as well
just keep churning.

00:25:57.062 --> 00:25:58.644
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, there's-- yeah.

00:25:58.644 --> 00:26:00.722
You don't need to do
anything special, really.

00:26:00.722 --> 00:26:01.680
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:03.744
Now, performance.

00:26:03.744 --> 00:26:05.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The performance--

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:06.118
it's quite interesting.

00:26:06.118 --> 00:26:11.820
Because it really depends
on what you're trying to do.

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.870
I'm going to go
first with Pub/Sub.

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:17.050
We actually have a
link to that, which is

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:19.300
you will get subsecond times.

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:22.059
Normally, you will get
milliseconds on both-- Task

00:26:22.059 --> 00:26:22.850
Queues and Pub/Sub.

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:23.240
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:24.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
But on Pub/Sub, you

00:26:24.740 --> 00:26:28.340
will get even less than a
second, even when you're

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:30.667
sending more than one
million messages per second.

00:26:30.667 --> 00:26:32.000
MARK MANDEL: That's pretty good.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:33.916
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
really good, actually.

00:26:33.916 --> 00:26:35.940
That means that it scales
really, really well.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:37.310
MARK MANDEL: Well, it
powers ads and Hangouts.

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:38.130
I mean, it has to, right?

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:38.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:39.663
So that's the thing.

00:26:39.663 --> 00:26:41.610
Pub/Sub will scale like crazy.

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:44.370
You can just go for
it and there will

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:46.140
be a little bit
of a degradation,

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:49.070
but it will never go up
over one second, which

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:50.200
is actually quite decent.

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:51.420
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:53.170
FRANCESC CAMPOY: On
Task Queues, though,

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:56.310
the scalability
won't be that big.

00:26:56.310 --> 00:27:00.190
So if you're trying to
send that many messages,

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:04.030
probably Pub/Sub could be a--
if you were doing big data with

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:07.530
a bunch-- imagine if you have
sensors in-- I don't know--

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:08.120
every--

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:09.369
MARK MANDEL: Every place ever.

00:27:09.369 --> 00:27:10.830
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Every place ever.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:12.560
That's a good place
to have sensors.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.180
And those sensors are actually
sending data all the time.

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:18.220
You have that much data
coming in-- probably

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:19.540
Pub/Sub is the way to go.

00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:21.780
MARK MANDEL: Pub/Sub
seems to be the way to go.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:23.238
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And finally, we'll

00:27:23.238 --> 00:27:25.070
also mention Cloud Monitoring.

00:27:25.070 --> 00:27:27.000
Cloud Monitoring--
as we said, Pub/Sub

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:30.430
allows you to have a
monitoring measure.

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:33.280
So you can-- the
things based on that.

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:37.070
Alerts and all the things,
like-- I forgot the word.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:39.070
MARK MANDEL: Say if you
were wanting to throttle

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:40.580
or you want to do autoscale.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:41.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Autoscale, that's it.

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:43.150
MARK MANDEL: Or that sort of fun
stuff and see what's going on.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:43.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If
you want to autoscale,

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.542
you can do that based on the
number of messages in Pub/Sub.

00:27:46.542 --> 00:27:48.500
MARK MANDEL: I actually
like the idea, as well,

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.477
of doing alerts,
which is kind of cool.

00:27:50.477 --> 00:27:52.310
If you hit past a certain
point, you'll be--

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:53.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
can toy with that

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:54.623
or you can do it the
other way around.

00:27:54.623 --> 00:27:57.041
You can say, if we're getting
less than 100 messages

00:27:57.041 --> 00:27:58.290
per second, something's wrong.

00:27:58.290 --> 00:27:58.750
MARK MANDEL: Something's wrong.

00:27:58.750 --> 00:27:59.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
just let me know.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:02.739
And you can do that very easily
from the monitoring console.

00:28:02.739 --> 00:28:04.655
MARK MANDEL: Can you do
that with Task Queues?

00:28:04.655 --> 00:28:08.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
could do it manually.

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:09.870
There's not a thing
that does that.

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:10.870
MARK MANDEL: Doesn't integrate.

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:11.220
OK.

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:12.136
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:12.136 --> 00:28:14.910
If you really want to do Task
Queues, and the only limitation

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:17.050
that you have is I would
like to have monitoring.

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:18.540
You could build
it on top of that.

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:19.790
There's not such a limitation.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:21.154
But you have to build it.

00:28:21.154 --> 00:28:21.820
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:22.450
Fair enough.

00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:23.362
All right.

00:28:23.362 --> 00:28:24.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:25.890
MARK MANDEL: Well I think
that probably covers

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:27.260
that topic quite succinctly.

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:29.194
Have you got anything
else you can think of?

00:28:29.194 --> 00:28:30.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Not really.

