00:28:59.505 --> 00:29:00.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:02.120
Cool.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:03.130
OK.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:03.860
Great.

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:06.937
Before we go, what is the
question of the week today?

00:29:06.937 --> 00:29:08.520
MARK MANDEL: The
question of the week.

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:11.150
It has to do with logging data.

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:13.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:13.850
More data.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:15.641
MARK MANDEL: I know
what people are saying.

00:29:15.641 --> 00:29:17.340
Look, I think it's interesting.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.670
Logging data is the
bread and butter.

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:21.226
It's the core of
anything you do.

00:29:21.226 --> 00:29:23.650
You need to know what
your application is doing.

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:24.540
It's super important.

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:27.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's even
less glamorous the messaging,

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:28.610
but even more
important, probably.

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:29.526
MARK MANDEL: Probably.

00:29:29.526 --> 00:29:30.950
Probably.

00:29:30.950 --> 00:29:34.256
Google Cloud Platform has
integrated logging platform.

00:29:34.256 --> 00:29:35.630
It allows you to
do some viewing.

00:29:35.630 --> 00:29:37.020
It's really awesome.

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:38.600
But we tend to only
hang around to it

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:40.270
for a limited amount of time.

00:29:40.270 --> 00:29:41.730
You can configure
it and say when

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:44.880
you want to drop data off and
do that sort of fun stuff.

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:49.100
But what happens if you want to
store your logs for all time?

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.420
Maybe you want to do some
big data analytics later on

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.860
or you just need it for
auditing purposes, or who knows?

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:56.960
There are so many
things you could do.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:58.766
Is there an easy way to do it?

00:29:58.766 --> 00:30:00.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I know there is.

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:00.780
MARK MANDEL: There is.

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:02.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I've never done it.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:04.394
So I'm going to let you
answer that question.

00:30:04.394 --> 00:30:05.435
MARK MANDEL: I love this.

00:30:05.435 --> 00:30:08.510
This got me so excited
when this came out.

00:30:08.510 --> 00:30:11.370
If you go into the
logging section, above it,

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:15.550
you should see-- in the
developer console in our web

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:17.800
app bin, there's a
section called Logs.

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:18.660
You go in there.

00:30:18.660 --> 00:30:20.326
And you'll also see
another thing called

00:30:20.326 --> 00:30:22.882
Exports, which is super cool.

00:30:22.882 --> 00:30:24.840
You can go in there, you
can select the service

00:30:24.840 --> 00:30:26.480
that you want to
export, and then

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:28.580
you basically have a one click.

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:30.890
Send it to a Google
Cloud Storage bucket

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:32.890
if you just want to store
for a really long time

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:34.460
and then do stuff with it.

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:37.490
But you can also send it to
BigQuery and you can also send

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:39.399
it to-- would you
believe it?-- Pub/Sub.

00:30:39.399 --> 00:30:40.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Amazing.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.890
MARK MANDEL: So if you want to
just stick it in a Google Cloud

00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:44.370
Storage bucket and then
do something with it

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.580
later-- you don't know what
you want to do-- that's fine.

00:30:46.580 --> 00:30:48.200
If you're really
doing analytics on it,

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.320
especially maybe
if you're doing--

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:51.242
say, if you've got
structured logs in JSON,

00:30:51.242 --> 00:30:52.200
that's really powerful.

00:30:52.200 --> 00:30:54.283
So you can send it straight
to BigQuery and stream

00:30:54.283 --> 00:30:54.960
it straight in.

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:56.935
So you can do
analytics on the fly.

00:30:56.935 --> 00:30:59.520
And then if you want to do
really crazy messaging stuff

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:01.200
with it, you can
send it to Pub/Sub,

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:03.550
which can then go to any
number of other places

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:04.560
from there, as well.

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:06.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's actually--

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:08.020
once you have it
on Pub/Sub, you can

00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:09.180
do whatever you want with it.

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:09.830
MARK MANDEL: It's crazy.

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:10.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:10.810
You already have it.

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:12.090
You can create
your subscriptions

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:12.340
and do whatever you want.

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:13.370
MARK MANDEL: We actually
didn't mention it,

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:15.950
but Pub/Sub integrates
directly with Dataflow,

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:17.420
one of our big data products.

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:20.044
So if you wanted your logs to be
set up to go through Dataflow,

00:31:20.044 --> 00:31:22.590
as well, that could be a really
interesting way of getting

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:23.840
all that data to flow through.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:24.210
It could be kind of cool.

00:31:24.210 --> 00:31:25.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Which is actually--

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.740
